Cell["7. Scope", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"04211bcc-c6a2-c24d-b08c-1acab1e1bed6"],

Cell["\<\
What is here is the extraction half of the framework: given derivations, \
factor each into a tracelet and a context, quotient by isomorphism, and \
report. That is Behr's characterisation theorem used in the direction that \
needs no new search, and it is why the implementation is a single pass over \
the tokens.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e565ea3a-f042-c349-828b-d63734ae3e3c"],

Cell["\<\
What is not here is the generative half: FETA, the algorithm that builds \
minimal traces ending in a chosen event by composing rules along admissible \
matches, without running the system at all. That needs an enumeration of the \
overlaps between the output of one rule and the input of the next, which is a \
real piece of work and a natural next step.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"376022b3-7fbf-9148-87fe-a5be4558265f"],

Cell["\<\
Two smaller limits. Abstraction equivalence is tested by canonicalising the \
two interfaces together, with each edge marked by the side it is on, \
alongside the rule sequence and the causal order; that is an invariant of the \
isomorphism class, and strong enough here, but it is not a proof of \
completeness. And overlaps are hyperedges only, as causality is everywhere in \
this stack \[LongDash] two steps meeting at a shared vertex and no shared \
edge count as independent.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"bb1446e6-a349-4545-ad68-c1cfedb0fdb1"]
},
